Objective Statement

Formal Objective

The Adaptive Vehicle Intelligence Architecture (AVIA) shall execute approved decisions by translating authorized decision representations into corresponding vehicle actions while maintaining the defined execution constraints, monitoring execution state, and reporting relevant execution outcomes.

---

Objective Intent

Establish the system capability required to transform an approved adaptive decision into physical or operational vehicle behavior without assuming responsibility for decision generation or authorization.

---

Success Criteria

This objective is considered satisfied when the system demonstrates the ability to:

- Receive an approved decision through the defined execution interface.
- Translate the approved decision into corresponding executable actions.
- Execute actions within applicable operational and engineering constraints.
- Monitor the state of action execution.
- Detect relevant execution deviations, incomplete execution, or execution failure.
- Report execution state and relevant execution outcomes.
- Preserve traceability between the approved decision and the resulting execution activity.

---

Engineering Constraints

- The objective shall remain solution-independent.
- Execution shall operate only on decisions that have satisfied the applicable authorization mechanism.
- SO-F-005 shall not independently authorize a generated decision.
- SO-F-005 shall not independently create a new adaptive decision as part of normal execution responsibility.
- Execution shall remain distinct from perception, contextual interpretation, and decision generation.
- Execution mechanisms shall operate within applicable safety, operational, and physical constraints.
- Execution failure shall be observable and reportable through appropriate system interfaces.
- A failure or deviation in execution shall not automatically grant the execution capability authority to redefine the intended decision.

---

Assumptions

- SO-F-004 provides the generated decision that enters the applicable authorization process.
- An approved decision is made available to SO-F-005 through an established interface.
- Applicable authority and authorization mechanisms will be established by the wider AVIA architecture.
- Vehicle execution mechanisms exist or will be derived from lower-level engineering requirements.
- Execution state can be observed sufficiently to determine whether an approved action is being performed as intended.
- Subsequent adaptive response to execution failure, where required, will be determined by the appropriate architectural capability.

---

Execution Boundary

The responsibility of SO-F-005 begins with an approved decision and ends with execution of the corresponding action and reporting of relevant execution state.

The objective does not determine what the vehicle should do next when execution fails, becomes impossible, or produces an unexpected result.

Such conditions shall provide information to the appropriate upstream or governing capability for subsequent determination.
